tage":-0.12594480978781675,"_gp_margin_advantage":false,"_score_margin_advantage":0.0,"_gate_pool_share":null,"_gp_pool_share":null,"_score_pool_share":null,"_gate_rev_durability":-0.12311776246149297,"_gp_rev_durability":false,"_score_rev_durability":0.0,"_gate_fcf_durability":0.04303375598337422,"_gp_fcf_durability":false,"_score_fcf_durability":46.5,"_gate_margins":0.0570886235054187,"_gp_margins":true,"_score_margins":100.0,"_gate_fund_growth":null,"_gp_fund_growth":null,"_score_fund_growth":0.0,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.025265333136954907,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":true,"_score_shrhldr_yield":39.2,"_gate_share_shrink":-0.007487641148642865,"_gp_share_shrink":true,"_score_share_shrink":59.4,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.0035505182090002437,"_gp_sbc_dilution":true,"_score_sbc_dilution":94.1,"_gate_insider_own":0.11908,"_gp_insider_own":true,"_score_insider_own":79.4,"_gate_insider_buying":0.857,"_gp_insider_buying":true,"_score_insider_buying":85.7,"pf":[],"description_full":"NGL Energy Partners LP engages in the crude oil, condensate, natural gas liquids, crude oil and produced water businesses in the United States. It operates through three segments: Water Solutions, Crude Oil Logistics, and Liquids Logistics. The Water Solutions segment transports, treats, recycles, and disposes produced and flowback water generated from crude oil and natural gas production; aggregates and sells recovered crude oil; disposes solids, such as tank bottoms, and drilling fluid and muds, as well as performs truck washouts; and sells produced water for reuse and recycle, and brackish non-potable water. The Crude Oil Logistics segment purchases crude oil from producers and marketers and transports it to refineries for resale at pipeline injection stations, storage terminals, barge loading facilities, rail facilities, refineries, and other trade hubs; and provides storage, terminaling, and transportation services through pipelines and storage tanks. The Liquids Logistics segment offers natural gas liquids to commercial, retail, and industrial customers across the United States and Canada through its five terminals, third-party storage and terminal facilities, nine common carrier pipelines and a fleet of leased railcars. This segment also provides services for marine exports of butane through its facility located in Chesapeake, Virginia; and owns a propane pipeline in Michigan. NGL Energy Holdings LLC serves as the general partner of the company. NGL Energy Partners LP was founded in 1940 and is headquartered in Tulsa, Oklahoma.","ceo_bio":"Mr. H. Michael Krimbill | President, CEO, & Director of NGL Energy Holdings LLC | Age 71 | Compensation: $2.6M (FY2026)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 449 people. Revenue per employee of $7.0M ranks in the top quartile among Energy peers, with revenue growing 6%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $322K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $25K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 0\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity).","financial_summary":["NGL ENERGY PARTNERS LP is a small-cap energy business with a $1.9B market cap on $3.2B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at -29% over the past three years and -12% annually across the full decade, and a shrinking top line is the hardest headwind any business can overcome.","Gross margins of 7% are the starting point for assessing pricing power.","The business throws off $145M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 5 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders.","On the balance sheet, debt is heavy at 8.9\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and leverage like that is what turns a bad year into a permanent loss, and thin 0.4\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield of 2.5% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insiders hold 12% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 the strongest alignment there is, and profit capture is only 0.3\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Piotroski score 3/9 \u2014 the fundamentals are deteriorating on multiple fronts","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Payout ratio of 300% leaves essentially no coverage if earnings wobble","sev":"red","cat":"dividend"},{"text":"Revenue growth in the bottom decile of Energy peers \u2014 losing share inside the profit pool","sev":"red","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Operating margins 13% below sector median \u2014 a cost disadvantage the moat cannot hide","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Carries a 30% premium to sector median EV/EBITDA (confirmed by 1 other valuation measure)","sev":"amber","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.30025810151993104},{"text":"Value-trap profile (score 52/100): leveraged balance sheet under pressure; structural revenue/margin decline \u2014 cheapness may reflect deterioration, not mispricing","sev":"amber","cat":"value_trap"},{"text":"Gross margin trails most Energy peers \u2014 competitors appear to have the pricing hand","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Carries more leverage than most Energy peers \u2014 a smaller cushion for the inevitable bad year","sev":"amber","cat":"balance_sheet"},{"text":"Geopolitical supply shocks \u2014 wars, sanctions, and OPEC decisions create volatility that no hedging programme can fully neutralise.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Insiders are net buyers (86% buy ratio) \u2014 insiders sell for many reasons but buy for only one","Large open-market insider purchases (net $0.7M) \u2014 management putting its own capital behind the business","Energy sector outperforming the broader market over the past quarter","Energy ETF in a sustained uptrend 